<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chertomlyk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th century BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chertomlyk is a royal Scythian burial mound of the 4th century BCE near Nikopol in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, about 20 metres high. Excavated by Ivan Zabelin in 1862–1863, it held the burials of a king, his attendants and horses, together with a gilded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> amphora decorated with scenes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> catching horses and a gold covering for a bow case. Its treasures are in the Hermitage Museum.</p></div>
